1
00:00:02,920 --> 00:00:07,195
In 1919, Candler's family sold
the company to a group of

2
00:00:07,195 --> 00:00:10,382
investors led by banker Ernest Woodruff.

3
00:00:10,382 --> 00:00:13,761
The price: twenty-five million dollars.

4
00:00:13,761 --> 00:00:17,148
A few years later, his son
Robert took over, and he ran

5
00:00:17,148 --> 00:00:19,273
Coca-Cola for decades.

6
00:00:19,273 --> 00:00:23,511
His goal was easy to say and hard
to do: put a Coke within an

7
00:00:23,511 --> 00:00:25,412
arm's reach of desire.

8
00:00:25,412 --> 00:00:28,587
That meant cartons that
carried six bottles home.

9
00:00:28,587 --> 00:00:30,662
Coolers at gas stations.

10
00:00:30,662 --> 00:00:34,750
Vending machines. And ads
everywhere, including the Christmas

11
00:00:34,750 --> 00:00:38,850
paintings of Santa Claus
that started in 1931.

12
00:00:38,850 --> 00:00:42,175
And no, Coca-Cola didn't
invent the red Santa.

13
00:00:42,175 --> 00:00:44,575
He was wearing red long before that.

14
00:00:44,575 --> 00:00:48,450
But those ads made a warm, jolly
Santa the one most people

15
00:00:48,450 --> 00:00:49,400
picture.

16
00:00:49,400 --> 00:00:51,487
Then came World War Two.

17
00:00:51,487 --> 00:00:56,538
In 1941, Woodruff made a promise:
every man in uniform would get

18
00:00:56,538 --> 00:01:00,150
a bottle of Coke for five
cents, wherever he was...

19
00:01:00,150 --> 00:01:02,239
and whatever it cost the company.

20
00:01:02,239 --> 00:01:05,914
To keep that promise, Coca-Cola
shipped bottling equipment

21
00:01:05,914 --> 00:01:10,239
overseas. During the war,
sixty-four bottling plants were set

22
00:01:10,239 --> 00:01:13,283
up, as close to the fighting as possible.

23
00:01:13,283 --> 00:01:16,808
Millions of soldiers came
home loving the drink.

24
00:01:16,808 --> 00:01:20,145
And the company came out of the war
with a head start in dozens

25
00:01:20,145 --> 00:01:25,100
of countries. Coca-Cola went
global by following the army.
